---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeHash from '@/components/tools/security/DevEncodeHash.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="sha1-hash-generator">
  <DevEncodeHash client:load algorithm="SHA-1" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What SHA-1 is</h2>
    <p>
      NIST published SHA-1 in 1995. It gives a 160-bit digest, written as 40 hexadecimal characters. For more
      than a decade it was the default hash of the web, in TLS certificates, Git object names, SSH
      fingerprints and code signing. It is now retired from every use where it must resist an attacker.
    </p>

    <h2>How SHA-1 was broken</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>2005</td><td>Theoretical attack published, well below brute force</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2011</td><td>NIST formally deprecates SHA-1 for digital signatures</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2017</td><td>SHAttered: Google and CWI publish two different PDFs with the same SHA-1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2020</td><td>Chosen-prefix collision demonstrated for roughly US$45,000 of compute</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2021</td><td>Browsers and OS vendors complete removal from certificate validation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      A chosen-prefix collision is the dangerous kind: the attacker chooses both documents in advance. That
      makes a forged signature possible.
    </p>

    <h2>Where SHA-1 is still used</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Git: object names are SHA-1. Git now uses a collision-detecting variant that rejects known attack
        patterns, and it supports SHA-256 repositories.
      </li>
      <li>
        HMAC-SHA1: collision attacks do not break HMAC. For this reason TOTP still specifies SHA-1 and all
        authenticator apps implement it.
      </li>
      <li>
        Legacy fingerprints: older SSH and PGP key fingerprints, kept for compatibility, not for security.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Git commit hashes will not match</h2>
    <p>
      A file hashed here gives a different value from <code>git hash-object</code>. Git does not hash only the
      file contents; it hashes a header first:
    </p>
    <pre><code>blob &lt;byte length&gt;\0&lt;contents&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      Commits, trees and tags each have their own header format. This prevents a blob and a tree with the
      same bytes from colliding in the object database.
    </p>

    <h2>Verifying an old download</h2>
    <p>
      Much archived software still lists only a SHA-1 sum. A SHA-1 check proves that the file was not
      truncated or corrupted in transit. It does not prove that nobody replaced the file, because an attacker
      who can do that can also edit the page that lists the hash. If a signature is available, verify the
      signature.
    </p>

    <h2>Move to SHA-256</h2>
    <p>
      For new work, use SHA-256: it has no known weaknesses, hardware acceleration on every modern CPU and
      universal support. The comparison table on this page shows the SHA-256 digest of the same input.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
